@page "/topbar"

<CatHead Title="Topbar and user" Tier="@CatHead.Tier1">
    The header strip: the search, the status and version chips on the right, and the user widget
    with its menu.
</CatHead>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Topbar.VersionTag" Title="Version tag" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--flush">
    <p>
        <code>.topbar-version</code> — which build the reader is looking at, in the topbar's right
        cluster beside the status indicator. Put a version the app already knows in it: an
        assembly informational version, a CI build number, a short commit sha; an
        <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> to a release page carries the class as well as the
        <code>&lt;span&gt;</code> does.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Topbar.HeaderSearch" Title="Header search" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--flush">
    <p>
        <strong>Live — type in it.</strong> <code>.search</code> goes at the start of the topbar,
        after the nav toggles and before <code>.topbar-spacer</code>, and is hidden below 720px, so
        an app needs another way in on a phone. <code>data-search</code> hands the box to
        <code>sednaUi.search</code>, which builds the result panel, ranks against the index the app
        registered and implements the keyboard; narrow it with
        <code>style="--search-max: 340px"</code> on the box, and keep the input's placeholder —
        the clear button is shown by CSS on <code>:placeholder-shown</code>.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatSnippet Name="Topbar/SearchResults.html" Title="Result panel">
    <p>
        One <code>.search-item</code> per result — a <code>.search-item-title</code> and a
        <code>.search-item-meta</code> line, with <code>.search-item--sel</code> where the keyboard
        is and <code>.search-status</code> for the busy and empty line. Put
        <code>.sedna-scroll</code> on the panel, use an <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> rather than a button
        so middle-click and &ldquo;open in a new tab&rdquo; work, and keep
        <code>role="option"</code> inside <code>role="listbox"</code> — that is what makes
        <code>aria-selected</code> legal while the box keeps focus through
        <code>aria-activedescendant</code>.
    </p>
</CatSnippet>

<CatSnippet Name="Topbar/AnchoredSearch.html" Title="A panel the app renders">
    <p>
        For an app searching a database itself: leave <code>data-search</code> off the input and
        add <code>.search-panel--anchored</code>, which nests the panel under the box —
        <code>.search</code> is already <code>position: relative</code>, so nothing is measured.
        <strong>The trade:</strong> a nested panel joins <code>.topbar</code>'s stacking context at
        z-index 60, so it sits <em>under</em> a modal backdrop however high its own z-index.
    </p>
</CatSnippet>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Topbar.UserMenu" Title="User menu" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--flush">
    <p>
        The panel is the library's one dropdown style, <code>.menu</code> — see
        <a href="/menu">Menus</a> for its items, labels and separators. Only the anchoring belongs
        to the frame, and <code>.user-widget</code> is <code>position: relative</code> so nothing
        is measured; opening it, the outside click and <kbd>Escape</kbd> are your app's state,
        <code>.menu-scrim</code> goes <em>immediately before</em> the panel because coming first in
        the markup is what puts the panel above it (the demo omits it), and do not declare
        <code>role="menu"</code> — the items are ordinary links and buttons that tab.
    </p>
</CatExample>
